  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 123 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nAs the cold front continues to track through the region on Tuesday, \nwrap around precipitation from the low pressure system should \nquickly wind down through the day, and mostly be out of the area\nby the late afternoon to early evening. Guidance continues to \ntrend towards an earlier arrival of the front, which should end \nprecipitation a bit earlier across portions of western Nebraska.\nWith the precipitation largely expected to be finished across \nwestern Nebraska by the morning, it now appears there will be \nvery little overlap with cooler air aloft, which should keep all\nprecipitation as rain. Will still keep an eye on the potential \nfor a little snow to mix in around Pine Ridge if the front \ntracks slower than guidance suggests. The main impacts will \nremain the strong, gusty northwest winds as the low remains in \nthe vicinity. Still expect to see sustained winds of 20 to 30 \nmph throughout the day, with gusts of 45 to 50 mph lasting \nthrough the evening. \n\nCooler, drier weather then settles in for the rest of the week. \nHighs are only expected to climb into the 50s through the weekend, \nwith lows in the lower to mid 30s. These highs are not unusual for \nearly November, as our typical highs start around 58 degrees, \ndropping to around 55 degrees by the end of the first week of \nNovember. Our lows are expected to remain slightly warmer than \nusual, as typically overnight lows the first week of November \nare around the mid 20s. \n\nBy the weekend, another upper level trough is expected to track \nacross the northern Plains, which may provide the forcing for \nanother surface system to bring precipitation across the region. At \nthis time, confidence remains low in placement and timing of the \ntrough, so confidence remains low for precipitation as well. \nHowever, will continue to keep an eye on this, as this system may \nbring our next chances for precipitation.",
